    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=10$.\n\nSet $u=\\log_{10}x$ and change to base 10; the other real solution lies between 0 and 1.",
      "worked": "The domain is $x>0$, $x\\ne1$. Let $x=10^u$ and $a=\\log_{10}2$, so $u\\ne0$, $\\log_{10}5=1-a$ and $\\log_{10}4=2a$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{u-(1-a)}{a}+\\frac{2a-1}{u}&=2a\\\\u(u-1+a)+a(2a-1)&=2a^2u\\\\u^2-(1-a+2a^2)u+a(2a-1)&=0\\\\(u-1)\\bigl(u-a(2a-1)\\bigr)&=0\\\\u&=1\\text{ or }a(2a-1).\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $1<2<\\sqrt{10}$, we have $0<a<\\tfrac12$. Thus $a(2a-1)<0$, and its corresponding value $x=10^{a(2a-1)}$ lies strictly between 0 and 1, so it is not an integer. For $u=1$, $x=10$.\n\nChecking in the original equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2\\frac{10}{5}+\\log_{10}\\frac25&=1+\\log_{10}\\frac25\\\\&=\\log_{10}10+\\log_{10}\\frac25\\\\&=\\log_{10}4.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, the integer solution is $x=10$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
